From 72f2e6e29bc4ad2425f699cd638968147f63e1a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 04:10:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Newton: relative tolerance of the linear solver adapted to
 the residual

Inexact Newton method (Eisenstat and Walker, 1996), as
mfem::NewtonSolver::SetAdaptiveLinRtol and the -snes_ksp_ew option of
PETSc: option AdaptiveLinearSolverTolerance of the Newton solver (None,
EisenstatWalker1 or EisenstatWalker2, with the options InitialTolerance,
MaximumTolerance, Gamma, Alpha and Threshold, defaults of MFEM). The
tolerance does not decrease faster than gamma eta^alpha above the
threshold, and is not smaller than half the ratio of the convergence
criterion to the norm of the residual (no oversolving, Kelley). The
relative tolerance of the linear solver is restored at the end of each
resolution (AdaptiveLinearSolverToleranceUpdater): the predictions keep
the tolerance given by the user. The linear solver shall be an iterative
solver of MFEM or a Krylov solver of hypre (refused otherwise on all the
processes); the tolerance used is added to the iterations information.

Utilities: getRelativeTolerance and setRelativeTolerance for the
iterative solvers of MFEM and the Krylov solvers of hypre;
getFactoryArgument (name, {name: options} or {Name, Options}) moved from
the fixed point solver to Parameters.

Tests: AdaptiveLinearSolverToleranceTest (tolerances of both choices
against a direct evaluation of the formulas, safeguards, restoration,
iterative solvers of MFEM and hypre, refusals), Newton with the conjugate
gradient on the cube of FixedPointSolverTest (97 to 104 iterations of the
conjugate gradient instead of 293, same solution). Mutations detected:
tolerance not set, not restored, no safeguard, no oversolving bound; the
norm of the linear residual of the first choice is not covered.
